owning a home has long been a staple of  the American Dream and at the center of  that are two big companies Fannie Mae  and Freddie Mac sound familiar maybe you  haven't thought that much about them  since the 2008 financial crisis the  federal government is stepping into it  to stabilize Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac  the two companies almost collapsed under  the weight of an unprecedented housing  bust but then the government stepped in  to bail them out now the Trump  administration has asked her plans to  reshape Fannie and Freddie but a housing  finance overhaul could mean privatizing  or shrinking the two companies a process  that some believe could mean mortgages  will become more expensive and less  affordable for millions of Americans  that includes the most popular home loan  in the u.s. the 30-year fixed-rate  mortgage homeowners like this mortgage  because of the low monthly payments and  the interest rate that doesn't fluctuate  we'll explain but first you have to  understand how Fannie and Freddie fit  into the u.s. housing system first home  owners borrow money from banks or other  lenders then Fannie and Freddie buy  these mortgages and package them into  securities then they sell those  securities to investors investors buy  them because they're considered safe  because Fannie and Freddie take on much  of the risk lenders and banks benefit  too since the government takes on much  of the default risk lenders can make  more loans to more people together  Fannie and Freddie now back about half  of new US mortgages so why do some want  to change the two companies  some in Washington want to shrink or  even get rid of Fannie and Freddie and  President Trump himself has indicated  that he wants to roll back the  government's involvement in the two  companies which have been under  government control since the financial  crisis but a housing finance overhaul  could mean privatizing or shrinking the  two companies those looking to shrink  Fannie and Freddie argue that the  government shouldn't be involved in the  housing market to the extent it is now  they say to the private sector not the  government should be filling this role  but those who support Fannie and Freddie  say that a home ownership is a path to  prosperity for the middle class and that  the government has a responsibility to  keep housing affordable but now some are  wondering what would the US housing  market look like without Fannie and  Freddie well to start with many non-bank  lenders might find it harder to operate  unlike banks these firms don't have  deposits which means it will be more  expensive for them to lend money and  Fannie and Freddie go away these so  called non-bank lenders make up about  half of new u.s. mortgages today and  they're often the only path that  first-time borrowers or middle-income  families have for getting a mortgage  that's because in the last decade many  banks have focused largely on wealthy  borrowers for making mortgages and the  non banks have moved in to fill the  space they left  beyond that no one's really sure what  the market would look like without  Fannie and Freddie but let's consider  other countries which don't operate  their housing markets like the US does  in many countries long-term fixed-rate  home loans including the 30-year  fixed-rate mortgage aren't largely  available instead mortgage rates reset  depending on the market and even a  one-point increase in your mortgages  interest rate can add hundreds of  dollars to your monthly payment we can  also look at the US before Fannie was  created in 1938 back then borrowers had  to come up with much larger down  payments often 50% or more mortgages  also tended to last for just five or ten  years with a big balloon payment that  came due at the end so future mortgages  could look like this or not at all much  of it depends on how the government  plans to reshape Fannie and Freddie  but reshaping them won't be easy people  like the benefits they create including  the 30-year fixed-rate mortgage and  right now there isn't much agreement on  either side of the aisle on how to get  rid of the government's involvement in  housing at least not without completely  overhauling the way the housing system  works so for now Fannie and Freddie's  future is still up in the air
 

